  "title": "Across and Through: trans-, per- and dia-",
  "short": "trans- means across or beyond; per- and dia- mean through.",
  "trouble": "per- often just strengthens a word, meaning thoroughly rather than through (perturb, persistent).",
  "prereqs": [
    "roots.inter-circum"
  ],
  "xp": 1,
  "viz": [
    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "practice": null,
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "These prefixes are about going across and through. <b>trans-</b> means “across” or “beyond”: to <i>transport</i> is to carry across, and to <i>transform</i> is to change beyond the old form. <b>per-</b> and Greek <b>dia-</b> mean “through”: to <i>perforate</i> is to pierce through, and a <i>diameter</i> measures straight through a circle."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-scene",
        "opts": {
          "scene": {
            "bg": "river",
            "cast": [
              {
                "who": "kai",
                "x": 52,
                "y": 11,
                "pose": "walk",
                "mood": "happy",
                "wear": [
                  "backpack"
                ],
                "face": "right"
              }
            ],
            "props": [
              {
                "kind": "bridge",
                "x": 50,
                "s": 1.2
              }
            ],
            "fx": [
              {
                "kind": "motion",
                "on": "c0",
                "at": "body",
                "dir": -1
              }
            ],
            "labels": [
              {
                "text": "TRANS = ACROSS",
                "x": 50,
                "y": 92
              }
            ],
            "alt": "A boy with a backpack walks across a bridge over a river."
          }
        },
        "cap": "A boy with a backpack walks across a bridge over a river."
      },
      {
        "t": "key",
        "html": "<b>trans-</b> = <b>across, beyond</b>. <b>per-</b> = <b>through</b>, and often <b>thoroughly</b>. <b>dia-</b> (Greek) = <b>through, across</b>."
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "<b>per-</b> has a second job. Often it does not mean “through” a place but “thoroughly,” making a word stronger: to <i>perturb</i> is to disturb thoroughly, and a <i>persistent</i> person stands firm all the way through."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "The parts and their words"
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-roots",
        "opts": {
          "max": 10,
          "roots": [
            "trans",
            "dia"
          ]
        },
        "cap": "trans- (across) and dia- (through)."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-roots",
        "opts": {
          "max": 10,
          "root": "per"
        },
        "cap": "Words on the list built on per- (through, thoroughly)."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "Words taken apart"
      },
      {
        "t": "table",
        "head": [
          "Word and meaning",
          "Parts, and what they say"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "<b>transient</b><br>lasting only a short time; passing through",
            "<b>trans-</b> <i>across</i> + i <i>go</i> + -ent<br>→ “going across (passing through)”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>perennial</b><br>lasting or returning year after year",
            "<b>per-</b> <i>through</i> + enn <i>year</i> + -ial<br>→ “lasting through the years”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>permeate</b><br>to spread through",
            "<b>per-</b> <i>through</i> + me <i>pass</i> + -ate<br>→ “pass through”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>pervasive</b><br>spreading through every part",
            "<b>per-</b> <i>through</i> + vas <i>go</i> + -ive<br>→ “going through everything”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>perspicacious</b><br>quick to notice and understand things",
            "<b>per-</b> <i>through</i> + spic <i>look</i> + -acious<br>→ “seeing through”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>perturb</b><br>to make anxious or upset",
            "<b>per-</b> <i>thoroughly</i> + turb <i>disturb</i><br>→ “thoroughly disturb”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>persistent</b><br>continuing firmly in spite of difficulty",
            "<b>per-</b> <i>thoroughly</i> + sist <i>stand</i> + -ent<br>→ “standing firm all the way”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>diatribe</b><br>a bitter verbal attack",
            "<b>dia-</b> <i>through</i> + trib <i>rub</i> + -e<br>→ “a rubbing through (a long, wearing attack)”"
          ],
          [
            "<b><i>diameter</i></b><br>a straight line through the center of a circle",
            "<b>dia-</b> <i>through</i> + meter <i>measure</i><br>→ “a measure through”"
          ]
        ],
        "cap": "Each word, what it means now, and its parts. Words in italics are familiar words that are not on the list."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "title": "Use the parts",
        "q": "The smell of baking bread <b>permeated</b> the whole house. What does <i>permeate</i> mean?",
        "steps": [
          "Take it apart: <b>per-</b> (through) + <b>mea</b> (pass) + <b>-ate</b> (verb ending).",
          "Literally: “to pass through.”",
          "The sentence fits: the smell passed through every room."
        ],
        "a": "To spread through."
      },
      {
